          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why is it hard to keep taking a preventer inhaler every day when you feel fine?",
          "answer": "Asthma is a chronic inflammatory condition of the airways. The airway lining is swollen, and the airways narrow easily. Symptoms and lung function vary from day to day, which is why you can feel fine for weeks. (Source: *Asthma*, general clinical reference.)\n\nThe sources I searched don't explain in detail how a preventer works inside the airway, or why its benefit is hard to notice. I can't fill that gap from memory. The closest point is that a check taken on a good day only shows how you were that day, and asthma can be present even when you feel well. (Source: *Asthma: confirming the diagnosis*, guideline.)\n\n**Practical barriers.** The adherence guidance says that taking an inhaler every day with poor technique can deliver much less medicine than it should, and that poor technique often goes unnoticed for years. Pressurized inhalers and dry powder inhalers each fail in different ways. Someone who uses both types has to do two contradictory things. (Source: *Sticking with asthma treatment*, lifestyle.) Ask a clinician or pharmacist to watch you use your device.\n\n**Tips for keeping up the habit.** The sources I searched contain general adherence advice, though it was written for diabetes rather than asthma. It suggests:\n- Tying doses to an existing daily routine rather than to a clock time.\n- Using reminders, a pill organizer or a blister pack.\n- Simplifying the regimen where possible.\n- Telling your clinician if cost is making you skip doses, since this is often not mentioned unless someone asks. (Source: *Sticking with a diabetes regimen*, lifestyle.)\n\n**Do not change your treatment on your own.** If the habit is hard to keep, or you are unsure whether you are using the inhaler correctly, speak to your clinician. They can review your treatment and technique with you.\n\n**Get urgent help now** if you have severe breathlessness, struggle to speak, or your chest is quiet during an attack. A quiet chest during a bad attack is a warning sign, not a sign of improvement. (Source: *Severe difficulty breathing*, red-flag guidance.)\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
